task: hole-scan
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/hole-scan
worktree: ../okay-wt-hole

why: `jiffy-hole-scan` says measure before redesigning. `Segments.pop`
  reports EMPTY when the head position is claimed but not published,
  so a producer descheduled in the two-instruction window between
  `tail.getAndIncrement()` and its publish hides every element behind
  it from the consumer. The question is how deep that cliff is on this
  runtime, because the fix (Jiffy's forward scan) needs a per-slot
  handled state and a lazily advancing head — a redesign of the
  consumer side of an MPMC buffer that already has a scar there.

what:
  1. a probe hook in `Segments`, test-only and null by default, called
     between the claim and the publish, so a test can hold a hole open
     for a known time and measure what the consumer does behind it.
  2. the natural cliff, no hook: `oneUnbounded_chunk` at 16 producers
     beside `oneRing_chunk`, minimum of five rounds.
  3. the decision, written either way: deep -> build the forward scan
     with the two-bit state; shallow -> close the entry saying so.

predict: a deliberate 5 ms hole stalls the consumer for the whole 5 ms
  with thousands of elements ready behind it (that part is certain
  from the code); the open question is the NATURAL rate, and my guess
  is that it is rare enough at 16 producers that `oneUnbounded_chunk`
  is within 10% of what it would be without holes — which would mean
  the two bits are not worth the redesign yet.
  Disqualifying: the unbounded lane trailing the bounded ring by more
  than 2x at 16 producers with no other explanation.
